* START-HISTORY:
* 18 Oct 26 dm S.65 write group: a third argument, writeok (the login is also in the Linux group sdfileswrite).  The requests that change state or
*           take a lock (7 to 10, 16 to 19, 30 to 33) are in the table as before but answer 0 unless writeok is true.
* 17 Oct 26 dm S.65 slice 2c: written.  The one place that decides what a files-only session (a user in sdusers and sdfiles but not in
*           sdapi; the SDNet login) may ask the API server for.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    ok = !apifilesok(action, entered, writeok)
*
*    action  - the request number, the Srvr codes of sdclient and the dispatch table of apisrvr.
*    entered - true when the session has already entered an account (apisrvr's master.login.done, set at the first
*              successful vb.account).  Request 3 (set account) is allowed only while it is false: the first entry is the
*              one after login; any later one would be an SDLogto, which a files-only session may not make.
*    writeok - true when the login is also in the Linux group sdfileswrite (apisrvr's files.write, read at login).  Off
*              unless the administrator of this server gave it (core direction 1).  The requests that change state or
*              take a lock are refused to a login without it: 7 to 10 (the locking reads), 16 to 19 (write, writeu, delete,
*              deleteu), 30 (lock record), 31 (clearfile), 32 (filelock) and 33 (fileunlock).  Reads, select, readv,
*              fileinfo, the indices, recordlocked and release (15) need sdfiles alone.  FAIL CLOSED: only exactly 1 (@true) is
*              "write"; empty, 0, 2 or text is "no write".  (Request 41, readv, is in the table for plain reads; its locking forms, flags 1, 2,
*              5 and 6, cannot be told from here because only the number is passed: apisrvr's vb.readv refuses them itself.)
*
*    Returns 1 when the request is allowed and 0 when it is not.  PURE: it reads nothing and writes nothing, so a
*    sandbox can ask it about every number.  FAIL CLOSED: a number not in the table below - empty, not a number, one
*    this tree does not know - is 0.  The table is an ALLOW list; a request added to apisrvr later is refused to a
*    files-only session until someone decides it belongs here.
*
*    Everything else about the session (the edit gates, the OS-access gate, SDCLIENT, the NET gate) applies unchanged:
*    this decides only which request numbers get as far as the dispatch.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function apifilesok(action, entered, writeok)
$catalog !apifilesok

* THE ALLOW TABLE.  One request number per line, the request in the comment.  A comma on each side so that the test
* below cannot match part of a number.  NOT IN THE TABLE, ON PURPOSE (all refused): 0 (no such request), 20 call,
* 21 execute, 22 respond, 23 end command, 24 and 25 the other logins, 27 and 28 (not supported), 29 open SDNet file,
* 47 and 48 (the SCRAM login itself, only before logged.in), 50 getvar, 51 system, 52 and 53 trans, 54 transactions,
* 55 to 60 evaluate and the conversions, 61 to 69 sequential files, and any number above 69.
files.ok = ','
files.ok := '1,'    ;* quit
files.ok := '2,'    ;* get extended error text
*                      3 set account is below: once only
files.ok := '4,'    ;* open file
files.ok := '5,'    ;* close file
files.ok := '6,'    ;* read
files.ok := '7,'    ;* readl
files.ok := '8,'    ;* readlw
files.ok := '9,'    ;* readu
files.ok := '10,'   ;* readuw
files.ok := '11,'   ;* select
files.ok := '12,'   ;* readnext
files.ok := '13,'   ;* clearselect
files.ok := '14,'   ;* readlist
files.ok := '15,'   ;* release
files.ok := '16,'   ;* write
files.ok := '17,'   ;* writeu
files.ok := '18,'   ;* delete
files.ok := '19,'   ;* deleteu
files.ok := '26,'   ;* selectindex (a select, so allowed with 37 to 39)
files.ok := '30,'   ;* lock record
files.ok := '31,'   ;* clearfile
files.ok := '32,'   ;* filelock
files.ok := '33,'   ;* fileunlock
files.ok := '34,'   ;* recordlocked
files.ok := '35,'   ;* indices1
files.ok := '36,'   ;* indices2
files.ok := '37,'   ;* selectlist
files.ok := '38,'   ;* selectindexv
files.ok := '39,'   ;* selectindexk
files.ok := '40,'   ;* fileinfo
files.ok := '41,'   ;* readv
files.ok := '42,'   ;* setleft
files.ok := '43,'   ;* setright
files.ok := '44,'   ;* selectleft
files.ok := '45,'   ;* selectright
files.ok := '46,'   ;* mark mapping
files.ok := '49,'   ;* revision

* THE CHANGE SET (18 Oct 26, S.65 write group): the numbers above that need writeok as well.  A subset of the table, by construction (the
* test above comes first).  netfile never sends 7 to 10 (a plain read is request 6, a lock is request 30), so refusing them breaks no read.
files.chg = ','
files.chg := '7,'    ;* readl
files.chg := '8,'    ;* readlw
files.chg := '9,'    ;* readu
files.chg := '10,'   ;* readuw
files.chg := '16,'   ;* write
files.chg := '17,'   ;* writeu
files.chg := '18,'   ;* delete
files.chg := '19,'   ;* deleteu
files.chg := '30,'   ;* lock record
files.chg := '31,'   ;* clearfile
files.chg := '32,'   ;* filelock
files.chg := '33,'   ;* fileunlock

if action = '' then return 0
if not(num(action)) then return 0

* 3 SET ACCOUNT: the first time only.
if action = 3 then
   if entered then return 0
   return 1
end

if not(index(files.ok, ',' : action : ',', 1)) then return 0

* 18 Oct 26 dm - S.65 write group.  Allowed by the table, but a request that changes state or takes a lock needs the write group as well.
*   The commas on each side again, so that 7 cannot match 17.  Same spelling as the table, so a number written another way ('16.0') was
*   refused above and never gets here.
*   FAIL CLOSED: only a writeok of exactly 1 (@true) lifts the change set; empty, 0, 2 and text all leave it refused.
if writeok # 1 then
   if index(files.chg, ',' : action : ',', 1) then return 0
end

return 1

end

* END-CODE
